# [WITAREA](../wg/witarea.html)

## Summary

The Web and Internet Transport Area (WITAREA) meeting at IETF 127 covered Area Director updates, a report and discussion on the SQUAD BOF, directorate reports (TSV-ART and HTTP Directorate), an extensive discussion regarding operational guidance requirements in IETF specifications, and a presentation on AI tooling for IETF participation and reviews.

## Key Discussion Points

### Area Overview and AD Updates
- Area Directors Gory Fairhurst and Mike Bishop presented the area status using the [Chair slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-witarea-chair-slides-00).
- Key milestones and updates:
  - 19 working groups and 2 directorates are active in the area.
  - Zaheduzzaman Sarker joined as Area Director responsible for NFS.
  - The PTHH BOF successfully progressed to a chartered working group.
  - Six RFCs were published since the previous meeting, crossing the RFC 10,000 milestone.

### SQUAD BOF Report
- Max Franke presented the outcome of the SQUAD BOF using the [SQUAD Report](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-witarea-squad-report-00) slides.
- The BOF brought together multicast experts (from MOPS, PIM, etc.) and transport developers to explore transport-layer support for one-to-many application semantics (leveraging underlying network-layer multicast evolutions such as BIER and SSM).
- **Technical & Architectural Discussion:**
  - **Application Interface vs. Transport Responsibilities:** Lars Eggert pointed out that QUIC does not define a standard application API. Max Franke clarified that the goal is to shift complexity to the sender side (congestion control, multicast decision logic) while keeping the receiver interface simple and standard.
  - **Transport Multiplexing:** Lucas Pardue noted similarities to QMux, providing common multiplexing capabilities in the transport domain.
  - **Framing & Scope:** Martin Thompson suggested ADs help frame outcomes for non-WG-forming BOFs to prevent over-fixation on specific draft solutions. Jonathan Hoyland noted the goal is enabling existing QUIC-based application interfaces to leverage one-to-many capabilities with minimal changes.
  - **Use Case Focus:** Tommy Pauly recommended focusing on concrete application use cases (e.g., large file downloads, media streaming) rather than focusing strictly on QUIC over IP multicast.

### Directorate Reports & Operational Guidance Discussion
- **TSV-ART Update:**
  - Magnus Westerlund reported on TSV-ART activities: 22 document reviews completed since the last IETF meeting. A new wiki page was added providing guidance for protocols intending to use QUIC.
- **Discussion on draft-ietf-opsawg-rfc5706bis:**
  - Extensive discussion occurred regarding draft-ietf-opsawg-rfc5706bis (Guidelines for Considering Operations and Management in IETF Specifications) and its proposed requirement to mandate an "Operations and Management Considerations" section in all IETF drafts.
  - Lars Eggert, Lucas Pardue, Martin Thompson, Mirja Kühlewind, and Tommy Pauly voiced strong concerns about mandatory sections, noting that many transport, HTTP, and web specifications do not fit operational management templates and forcing sections leads to irrelevant or fabricated text.
  - Gory Fairhurst explained the draft is currently under AD review to incorporate feedback from directorate and community reviews.
  - Mirja Kühlewind noted process concerns with applying sweeping mandatory requirements across all areas via IETF Last Call without earlier cross-area engagement.
- **HTTP Directorate Update:**
  - Mark Nottingham provided an update on HTTP Directorate activities, noting around 14 reviews completed in the last quarter and emphasizing guidance under BCP 56 (RFC 9205).
  - Common pitfalls highlighted by Mark Nottingham, Lucas Pardue, Matt Joras, and Mike Bishop include defining fixed URIs, unnecessarily tunneling protocols over HTTP POST, ignoring HTTP intermediaries (CDNs, proxies, load balancers), and assuming rigid HTTP status code behaviors. Early directorate review was strongly urged.

### AI Is Here to Help
- Mark Nottingham presented [AI Is Here to Help](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-witarea-ai-is-here-to-help-00), detailing open-source tooling (`IETF LLM` and `IETF skill`) designed to assist participants and reviewers.
- **Features & Capabilities:**
  - Semantic search and full-text access across the RFC series, section-level lookup, obsoletes/updates tracking, and IMAP-based mailing list corpus indexing.
  - Integration of session transcripts linked to DataTracker identities.
  - Markdown-based skill definitions to guide LLMs on IETF norms, consensus semantics, contribution etiquette, and document review rules (including BCP 56 bis for HTTP).
- **Discussion & Feedback:**
  - **Human-in-the-Loop:** Mark Nottingham and Mirja Kühlewind emphasized that AI tools should generate review fodder or self-review insights, but human judgment remains essential before submitting reviews or list contributions.
  - **Self-Review & Telechat Prioritization:** Lucas Pardue noted positive experiences using the tool for self-reviewing drafts (e.g., Qlog/QUIC specs). Mike Bishop described using the tool to triage IESG Telechat agendas.
  - **Integration Possibilities:** Dan Romascanu suggested broader community documentation and incorporating meeting VCon audio/video transcript data. Alessandro Ghedini and Magnus Westerlund discussed integrating alternate discussion sources (Slack/Zulip) and GitHub issue tracking.

## Decisions and Action Items

- **SQUAD Mailing List:** A dedicated `squad` mailing list will be established to continue technical discussions on one-to-many transport semantics and coordinate with related efforts (e.g., MoQ).
- **draft-ietf-opsawg-rfc5706bis Feedback:** WIT AREA participants and ADs will continue to track and provide feedback on updates to draft-ietf-opsawg-rfc5706bis.
- **Early HTTP Directorate Engagement:** Authors designing protocols on top of HTTP are instructed to request reviews early in the design process.

## Next Steps

- SQUAD proponents to refine technical problem statements, engage with the MoQ working group community, and identify operators willing to support deployment before pursuing a working group charter.
- Mark Nottingham to continue refining `IETF LLM` and `IETF skill` tooling based on community feedback.